>
  <p>Zaaheen is an independent AI consultancy based in Dubai that assesses businesses of twenty
     staff or more across the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman, on site and
     role by role. We sell and build no tools, so this guide is written from the buyer's side of
     the table.</p>

  <section id="honest-list">
    <h2>Is there an honest list of the best AI consultancies in the UAE?</h2>
    <p>No, and you should be wary of any page that claims to be one. Most "top AI consulting
       companies in Dubai" lists are paid placements, directories that rank whoever buys the top
       slot, or pages a consultancy wrote about itself and put first.</p>
    <p>Even an honest list could not answer your real question. Whether a consultant suits you
       depends on your business: how many teams you have, how your work is done, what data you
       hold and what you can afford to change. Nobody can rank that from the outside.</p>
    <p>So instead of a list, this guide gives you the checks to run yourself, whether you are
       looking for an AI consultant in Dubai, Abu Dhabi or Riyadh, or anywhere else in the Gulf.
       The checks are the same everywhere in
       the GCC.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why does the choice matter so much for Gulf businesses right now?</h2>
    <p>Because most Gulf companies already plan to use AI, and far fewer are set up to make it
       work. Roland Berger's Middle East report of 10 February 2026 found the gap plainly:</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-gcc-ai-gap"
      title="GCC organisations with AI plans, against those with the means to deliver them, per cent, 2026"
      measure="Per cent of GCC organisations"
      max={100}
      bars={[
        { label: 'AI in strategic plans', value: 80 },
        { label: 'Data strategy', value: 33 },
        { label: 'Model to scale AI', value: 33 },
        { label: 'AI ethics board', value: 28 },
      ]}
      source={{ text: 'Roland Berger, Middle East report "4 in 5 GCC organizations have an AI strategy", 10 February 2026. Sample size not stated. "Fewer than one in three" is drawn at 33%.', href: ROLAND_BERGER }}
    />

    <p>In the report's own words: "nearly 80%" of GCC organisations have AI in their strategic
       plans, "only one-third" have an enterprise-wide data strategy, "fewer than one in three"
       have the operating model to scale it, and "only 28%" have an AI ethics or compliance
       board.</p>
    <p>The first action the same report recommends is an organisation-wide AI readiness
       assessment. That makes the person you hire to do it one of the most important choices in
       the whole project.</p>
    <p>The wider research points the same way. Boston Consulting Group found in October 2024
       that 74% of companies had yet to show tangible value from AI, and that the leaders put
       about 70% of their effort into people and processes rather than technology
       (<a href={BCG}>BCG, 1,000 executives in 59 countries</a>). RAND's August 2024 study found
       the most common cause of failure is that people "misunderstand, or miscommunicate, what
       problem needs to be solved using AI" (<a href={RAND}>RAND, 65 interviews</a>). A consultant
       who starts with the tool, not the problem, walks straight into that. We cover the
       pattern in more depth in <a href="/learn/why-ai-projects-fail/">why AI projects fail</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="check-first">
    <h2>What should you check first?</h2>
    <p>Check whose side the consultant is on, and whether they will see your real work before
       they advise you. Everything else follows from those two. Use this table when you compare
       anyone, from a large firm to a one-person practice:</p>

    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">What a good answer looks like</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Independence</th><td>They sell, resell and build nothing, and their only income is your fee</td><td>Otherwise every recommendation has a hidden reason behind it</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sees the real work</th><td>They come on site and sit with the people doing it, not only the managers</td><td>The workarounds that decide whether AI fits are invisible from a meeting room</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A clear method</th><td>They can explain how they map, time and score each step</td><td>Without a method, the advice is opinion</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Honest verdicts</th><td>They will tell you where not to use AI, and where to fix the process first</td><td>An adviser who always says "AI" is selling, not advising</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Written scope and price</th><td>A fixed quote in writing, with what each stage covers</td><td>Open-ended day rates and "phase two" surprises are where budgets go</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Data handling</th><td>A confidentiality agreement before the first visit, and a date when notes are deleted</td><td>They will see how your business runs, sometimes with customer data on screen</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who does the work</th><td>The people you meet are the people who visit and write the report</td><td>Senior people at the pitch and juniors on site is a common swap</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>For a smaller business, the first two checks matter most. If you have thirty staff, one
       wrong tool bought on a sales pitch can cost you a year of effort. If you are still unsure
       what a proper assessment covers, read
       <a href="/learn/ai-readiness-assessment-uae/">what an AI readiness assessment in the UAE covers</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="questions">
    <h2>Which questions should you ask an AI consultant?</h2>
    <p>Ask these five questions in the first call, and ask for the answers in writing. A
       genuine adviser will answer each one in a sentence.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Do you sell, resell or build any AI tools?</strong> If yes, their advice will
          tend to lead to their own product, even with the best intentions.</li>
      <li><strong>Do you take commissions, referral fees or kickbacks from any provider?</strong>
          Many software companies pay partners for each customer they bring. Ask directly; it is a
          normal question.</li>
      <li><strong>Are you a partner or reseller of any provider?</strong> Partner badges look
          reassuring on a website. They also mean a commercial tie.</li>
      <li><strong>Will you come on site and watch the work being done?</strong> A recommendation
          made from a questionnaire or a single workshop is a guess about your business.</li>
      <li><strong>Will you tell us where not to use AI?</strong> Ask for an example of work they
          would leave alone, or would solve with plain automation instead.</li>
    </ol>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-five-questions"
      from="Vendor shortlist"
      to="Independent advice"
      fact="No commissions taken"
      hub="Five questions"
      hubNote="tools? fees? on site?"
      saved="Checked"
      caption="Five questions, asked in the first call, separate an adviser who works for you from a seller who works for a supplier."
    />

    <p>Two follow-up questions are worth asking too: what you get at the end, and what happens to
       the notes they take about your business. You can see how we answer all of these on our
       <a href="/consulting/#questions">AI Readiness Assessment questions</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="red-flags">
    <h2>What are the red flags?</h2>
    <p>The clearest red flag is a recommendation made before anyone has seen your work. Watch for
       these in Dubai, Abu Dhabi, Riyadh or anywhere else in the Gulf:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A free AI audit that leads into their own build work.</strong> The audit is
          the sales step, and the answer is usually the product or project they sell.</li>
      <li><strong>Commissions or referral fees.</strong> If they earn from the tool you buy, they
          are not neutral about which tool you buy.</li>
      <li><strong>Partner or reseller ties.</strong> A wall of supplier logos tells you who pays
          them, as well as who trained them.</li>
      <li><strong>A tool-first pitch.</strong> If the first meeting is a demonstration of a
          product, the conclusion was decided before the question. Tools chosen from a demo,
          without seeing the real work, tend to end up unused.</li>
      <li><strong>No site visit.</strong> An AI audit delivered in a day or two, without anyone
          coming to your office, has not seen how your people really work.</li>
      <li><strong>A readiness quiz sold as an assessment.</strong> An online quiz scores your own
          answers about yourself. It cannot see the hand-offs, waiting and rework that decide
          where AI helps.</li>
      <li><strong>Savings promised before the visit.</strong> Nobody can promise hours saved
          before they have timed the work.</li>
      <li><strong>No written quote.</strong> If the price is vague at the start, it rarely gets
          smaller later.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of these prove bad intent. They tell you whose interests the advice is built around.
       The difference between a task that needs AI and one that needs a simple rule is often the
       first thing a seller blurs; our guide to
       <a href="/learn/ai-vs-automation/">AI vs automation</a> explains it plainly.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="adviser-or-builder">
    <h2>Should you hire an adviser or a company that builds AI?</h2>
    <p>Hire an adviser to decide what to do, and a builder to do it, ideally as two separate
       decisions. A company that builds AI systems is often good at building. Its view of what
       you need will still lean towards what it builds.</p>
    <p>An independent adviser has nothing to gain from any answer, so "automate this with a simple
       form", "fix the process first" or "leave this team alone" are all possible outcomes. Once
       you know what to do and what it should cost, you can brief a builder or buy a product
       directly from its maker, with a clear scope they cannot quietly expand.</p>
    <p>Before you spend on either, it helps to know how to judge the return. Our guide to
       <a href="/learn/ai-roi-before-you-buy/">working out AI ROI before you buy</a> sets out the
       sums.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-zaaheen">
    <h2>What makes Zaaheen different?</h2>
    <p>We built <a href="/consulting/">our AI Readiness Assessment</a> around the checks in this
       guide. What that means in practice:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>On site, every role.</strong> We walk the business from reception to the
          managing director, sit with the people doing the work and time each step.</li>
      <li><strong>Independent.</strong> We do not sell, resell or build any AI product, and we
          take no referral fees, commissions or kickbacks. Our only income is the fee you pay us.</li>
      <li><strong>Four honest verdicts.</strong> Every team gets one: AI now, Automate without AI,
          Fix the process first, or Leave as it is. Some work should stay with your people, and we
          say so.</li>
      <li><strong>Twenty years in operations.</strong> Our team spent twenty years in corporate
          operations: customer service, back office, sales and team management.</li>
      <li><strong>Numbers for every recommendation.</strong> The effort, a cost range, the hours
          saved each month, when it pays back, and where a person must stay in charge, in a
          written report and a read-out with your team.</li>
      <li><strong>A clear price.</strong> A free 30-minute call, then a fixed quote in writing,
          with no hidden costs, plus VAT where it applies.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If that is the kind of adviser you are looking for, start with an
       <a href="/consulting/">AI readiness assessment in Dubai</a>, or wherever in the Gulf your
       offices are.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>Common questions about choosing an AI consultant in the GCC</h2>

    <h3>How do I find an AI consultant in Dubai or Saudi Arabia?</h3>
    <p>Start with a short list from referrals and search, then run the five questions above on
       a first call. Rule out anyone who sells tools, takes commissions or will not come on site.
       The ones left can be judged on method, written scope and price.</p>

    <h3>Do AI consultants take commissions?</h3>
    <p>Some do. Many software providers run partner and referral programmes that pay for each
       customer brought to them. Ask any consultant directly, and get the answer in writing. We
       take none: our only income is the fee you pay us.</p>

    <h3>What is the best AI consulting for small businesses?</h3>
    <p>There is no single best; the right fit is an independent adviser who looks at your actual
       work and gives you a fixed price. For a business of twenty to a hundred people, a focused
       on-site assessment of the teams that matter most is usually more useful than a broad
       strategy document.</p>

    <h3>Is a free AI audit worth it?</h3>
    <p>It can be a useful conversation, but check what follows it. If the "audit" leads straight
       into the provider's own build work or product, treat it as a sales meeting. A real
       assessment means someone watching and timing the work on site, which takes days, not
       minutes.</p>

    <h3>Do you come to offices in Abu Dhabi, Riyadh and Doha?</h3>
    <p>Yes. We are based in Dubai and do the work on site across the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Qatar,
       Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman. The timeline is agreed in writing before we start.</p>

    <h3>What does an AI assessment cost in the UAE?</h3>
    <p>It depends on how many teams and sites are involved, so we quote after the free call. The
       quote is fixed, in UAE dirhams plus VAT where it applies, and in writing, and nothing is
       added later without your written agreement.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
